{"step_index":467,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-21T23:14:55Z","thinking":"**Investigating Drag and Drop**\n\nI've identified the drag-and-drop zones as `#zone-shortage` and `#zone-warehouse`. I was planning to add listeners, but I discovered that pywebview has a native override for file drops, unless we configure `drag_drop=False`. It looks like Edge on Windows blocks file dropping by default too. I need to figure out a workaround.\n\n\n**Implementing JS Drag and Drop**\n\nI've decided to enable JavaScript drag-and-drop to identify the drop zone, and I'm adding `dragover`, `dragleave`, and `drop` event listeners to the zones. On drop, I'll extract file paths and pass them to Python if available. I'm taking care to account for browsers/pywebview that might not expose file paths, so I'm filtering for the `.path` property. The event listener code is being finalized now.\n\n\n**Refining Event Handling**\n\nI've realized that Edge Webview2 doesn't natively expose `File.path` for security reasons. Therefore, using JavaScript drag-and-drop isn't the best approach for this task. I'm now focusing on utilizing `pywebview`'s `window.events.dropped` API, which directly provides a list of absolute file paths to Python. The challenge now is to determine *where* the files were dropped on the JS side, to update the file lists accordingly.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"replace_file_content","args":{"AllowMultiple":false,"Description":"Add log viewer to Settings screen","EndLine":857,"Instruction":"Add a log viewer section to the screen-settings div","ReplacementContent":"            <!-- Screen 3: Settings -->\n            <div id=\"screen-settings\" class=\"screen\">\n                <header class=\"page-header\">\n                    <h2><i class=\"ph-fill ph-gear\"></i> إعدادات النظام</h2>\n                    <p>قم بضبط إعدادات الربط مع سيرفر الذكاء الاصطناعي (MSEMAX) و Unstructured API.</p>\n                </header>\n\n       